WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.800
Marine threespine sticklebacks have a pelvis with two spines.

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.460
In Paxton Lake, the bottom-feeding fish have a greatly reduced pelvis, and most have

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:13.780
no pelvic bones at all.

4
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.740
The gene most strongly tied to the difference is Pitx1.

5
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.080
Yet its protein is unchanged in the fish examined.

6
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.800
How can a body part shrink while its gene still makes a normal protein?

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:32.463
Pitx1 makes a protein that binds DNA, and mice without it have malformed hind limbs.

8
00:00:33.323 --> 00:00:36.063
But a gene can have several switches, called enhancers.

9
00:00:37.083 --> 00:00:42.023
An enhancer is a short stretch of noncoding DNA that raises a gene's use in

10
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:43.023
particular tissues.

11
00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.423
One switch can serve the jaw, another the pelvis.

12
00:00:46.423 --> 00:00:49.883
Break the pelvic switch, and the jaw light stays on.

13
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:54.757
Mapping found a region near the gene, but a region can hold many genes.

14
00:00:55.617 --> 00:01:00.417
The change might sit beside the gene, in cis, or in another gene that supplies its

15
00:01:00.417 --> 00:01:01.737
regulators, in trans.

16
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:04.457
A hybrid can test for a cis change.

17
00:01:05.317 --> 00:01:09.797
Each hybrid cell holds a marine allele and a Paxton allele, two versions of the

18
00:01:09.797 --> 00:01:11.157
gene, in one nucleus.

19
00:01:11.157 --> 00:01:13.997
Both meet the same transcription factors.

20
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:19.710
In an invented example, ninety percent of pelvic transcripts come from the marine

21
00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.940
allele. Ten percent come from the Paxton allele, and in the jaw the split is fifty

22
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:30.730
fifty. The factors are shared, so the Paxton allele itself is less active: a change

23
00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.990
in cis, in the pelvis but not the jaw.

24
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:37.310
In 2010, Chan and colleagues ran the real version in hybrids.

25
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:41.990
The Paxton allele was lower in pelvic tissue, but not in head tissue.

26
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:46.773
That study also found Pel, an enhancer upstream of the gene.

27
00:01:47.713 --> 00:01:52.573
In a reporter assay, a sequence is joined to a visible marker and placed in embryos.

28
00:01:53.973 --> 00:01:58.233
Marine Pel drove pelvic expression, but the Paxton version, which carried a

29
00:01:58.233 --> 00:02:01.633
deletion, gave none in eight transgenic fish tested.

30
00:02:02.393 --> 00:02:06.873
A reporter shows only what a sequence can drive, not that form depends on it.

31
00:02:07.473 --> 00:02:13.593
Rescue comes closer: a marine Pel Pitx1 minigene variably enhanced pelvic spines in

32
00:02:13.593 --> 00:02:14.593
Bear Paw Lake fry.

33
00:02:15.583 --> 00:02:21.643
Deleting the enhancer PelB in mice left about eighty-five percent of hind-limb Pitx1

34
00:02:21.643 --> 00:02:27.923
RNA. Several bones were shorter, and this enhancer supplies part of the limb's Pitx1

35
00:02:27.923 --> 00:02:31.023
expression, with other enhancers supplying the rest.

36
00:02:32.003 --> 00:02:36.783
Mapping localises, in situ hybridization shows where the transcript is, and the

37
00:02:36.783 --> 00:02:38.703
hybrid test detects a cis difference.

38
00:02:39.523 --> 00:02:44.303
A reporter shows what a sequence can drive, and a native deletion tests whether form

39
00:02:44.303 --> 00:02:47.503
depends on it. Each method answers its own question.
